          "type": "turn",
          "question": "At what blood pressure reading is hypertension diagnosed, and do the major bodies agree?",
          "answer": "Hypertension, or high blood pressure, is diagnosed based on repeated measurements rather than a single reading. It is classified according to certain guidelines. According to the United States society guidance, the classifications are as follows:\n\n- **Normal**: Systolic below 120 mm Hg and diastolic below 80 mm Hg.\n- **Elevated**: Systolic 120 to 129 mm Hg and diastolic below 80 mm Hg.\n- **Stage 1 Hypertension**: Systolic 130 to 139 mm Hg or diastolic 80 to 89 mm Hg.\n- **Stage 2 Hypertension**: Systolic at least 140 mm Hg or diastolic at least 90 mm Hg.\n\nWhen systolic and diastolic numbers fall into different categories, the higher category applies (source: guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets).\n\nMajor health organizations tend to have similar thresholds for the diagnosis of hypertension, focusing on sustained blood pressure readings rather than isolated measurements (source: condition-hypertension).\n\nWhat I could not cover is specific agreement among all major health organizations, as this summary does not provide that detail. You may want to look up each organization's guidelines for a comprehensive understanding.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
